# Understanding Polar Functions

Polar functions represent curves where each point is determined by a distance from the origin (r) and an angle (θ) from the positive x-axis. Let me help you understand this concept with clear explanations and visualizations.

## Basic Polar Function Concepts

### 1. Polar Coordinates System
In polar coordinates, each point is defined by:
- r: The distance from the origin (radius)
- θ: The angle from the positive x-axis (in radians or degrees)

### 2. Basic Polar Graphs

#### Circle Centered at Origin
The equation: r = a (constant)
```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=3.14mm]{standalone}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape, baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
    % Draw polar grid
    \foreach \r in {1,2,3} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) circle (\r);
    }

    % Draw angle lines
    \foreach \a in {0,30,...,330} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) -- (\a:3.5);
    }

    % Draw axes
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {x};
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {y};

    % Draw circle
    \draw[thick, blue] (0,0) circle (2);
    \node[blue, right] at (2,0) {r = 2};

    % Add labels
    \node at (0,3.5) {Polar Coordinates};
    \node at (0,3) {Circle: r = constant};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

#### Line Through Origin
The equation: θ = a (constant)
