### 3. Common Polar Functions

#### Cardioid
The equation: r = a(1 ± cosθ) or r = a(1 ± sinθ)
```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=3.14mm]{standalone}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape, baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
    % Draw polar grid
    \foreach \r in {1,2,3} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) circle (\r);
    }

    % Draw angle lines
    \foreach \a in {0,30,...,330} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) -- (\a:3.5);
    }

    % Draw axes
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {x};
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {y};

    % Draw cardioid
    \draw[thick, purple, domain=0:2*pi, samples=200, smooth, variable=\t] plot ({cos(\t r)}, {sin(\t r)*(1+cos(\t r))});
    \node[purple, right] at (1,1.5) {r = 1 + cosθ};

    % Add labels
    \node at (0,3.5) {Polar Coordinates};
    \node at (0,3) {Cardioid: r = 1 + cosθ};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

#### Rose Curve
The equation: r = a sin(nθ) or r = a cos(nθ)
```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=3.14mm]{standalone}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
