    % Draw polar grid
    \foreach \r in {1,2,3} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) circle (\r);
    }

    % Draw angle lines
    \foreach \a in {0,30,...,330} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) -- (\a:3.5);
    }

    % Draw axes
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {x};
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {y};

    % Draw rose curve
    \draw[thick, orange, domain=0:2*pi, samples=200, smooth, variable=\t] plot ({cos(\t r)*sin(3*\t r)}, {sin(\t r)*sin(3*\t r)});
    \node[orange, right] at (1,1.5) {r = sin(3θ)};

    % Add labels
    \node at (0,3.5) {Polar Coordinates};
    \node at (0,3) {Rose Curve: r = sin(3θ)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

## Converting Between Polar and Cartesian Coordinates

### Conversion Formulas:
- Cartesian to Polar:
  - r = √(x² + y²)
  - θ = arctan(y/x)

- Polar to Cartesian:
  - x = r cosθ
  - y = r sinθ

### Example Conversion:
Let's convert the polar point (3, 45°) to Cartesian coordinates:
```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=3.14mm]{standalone}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape, baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
    % Draw polar grid
    \foreach \r in {1,2,3} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) circle (\r);
    }

    % Draw angle lines
    \foreach \a in {0,30,...,330} {
        \draw[thin, gray] (0,0) -- (\a:3.5);
    }

    % Draw axes
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {x};
    \draw[thick, ->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {y};

    % Draw point in polar coordinates
    \filldraw[blue] (45:3) circle (2pt) node[above right] {(3, 45°)};

    % Draw conversion to Cartesian
    \draw[dashed, blue] (0,0) -- (45:3);
    \draw[dashed, blue] (45:3) -- (45:3 |- 0,0) node[below] {x ≈ 2.12};
    \draw[dashed, blue] (45:3) -- (45:3 -| 0,0) node[left] {y ≈ 2.12};

    % Add labels
    \node at (0,3.5) {Polar to Cartesian Conversion};
    \node at (0,3) {(3, 45°) → (2.12, 2.12)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

## Graphing Polar Functions

When graphing polar functions, remember:
1. Start at θ = 0 and move counterclockwise
2. Consider the periodicity of trigonometric functions
3. Watch for asymptotes and undefined points

